-- Regression fixture for issue #920, second half.
--
-- Two DIFFERENT types both spelled `Verdict`. Nothing here names the type, so
-- nothing is ambiguous: the constructors `yes` and `maybe` are distinct names,
-- and each pins down its own `Verdict`. The mismatches below are therefore
-- genuine, and must be reported -- once for the branches of an IF, once for the
-- patterns of a clause group, which is rendered by a different message.
--
-- What was broken is only how they READ. `prettyLayout` renders a type from its
-- surface names and never shows uniques, so a message came out as
--
--   From looking at the context, I have inferred that the type of this
--   definition must be
--     Verdict
--   but is here of type
--     Verdict
--
-- which tells the reader nothing at all. The golden must now show each side
-- qualified by where its `Verdict` was declared.

DECLARE Verdict IS ONE OF yes, no

DECLARE Verdict IS ONE OF maybe, perhaps

GIVEN x IS A BOOLEAN
`pick` MEANS IF x THEN yes ELSE maybe

GIVETH A NUMBER
`score` yes MEANS 1
`score` maybe MEANS 2
